files.summary:
The complete database spec (DB-01 to DB-05), owned by R2 with R5 as reviewer. It covers:
- an ERD in Mermaid, normalisation reasoning, and deviations from the §11.1 reference schema;
- the adjacency-list tree decision, with pilot numbers;
- the full DDL for all 17 canonical tables as five dbmate migrations, with constraints, indexes, grants, the acyclicity trigger (SQLSTATE PTC01), the P0001 fault hook, may_r/may_x and the canonical checksum function;
- the READ COMMITTED justification with pilot evidence, the L0–L4 lock order, lockPlan() and a Kysely sketch of vfsTxn() with retries, timeouts, cancellation and the SQLSTATE→errno mapper;
- set-based SQL for rm -r, cp -r, mv, batch rename (two-phase for swaps), chmod -R, chown -R, create and write/CAS, all with quota updates;
- the fault-injection harness rules and what counts as a node op;
- op_log and the per-owner feed, retention and tombstones; auth_sessions vs os_sessions; S/X leases with fencing; swap_pages, blobs and GC, file_chunks and pgvector, the LLM budget tables, audit_log and telemetry;
- Q1–Q5 SQL with expected plans, the full index list and the perf-index toggle scripts;
- the seed and benchmark protocol, integrity probes P1–P9, and backup/restore using an exported snapshot plus a drill;
- PostgreSQL settings, initdb args and Kysely/codegen conventions;
- invariants with proof sketches, performance budgets and about 100 test titles that start with requirement IDs.

The DDL and SQL were run for real on PostgreSQL 18.6 + pgvector 0.8.6 (image 0.8.6-pg18) in a throwaway container:
- the migration up/down/up round trip gives an identical schema;
- 40 DB-04 constraint probes behave as intended;
- fault matrices for every bulk op roll back cleanly;
- the concurrent-move race is stopped by the trigger;
- Q1–Q5 plans were checked at 100k inodes, before and after indexes;
- backup and restore reproduce the checksum;
- all 48 statements compile with PREPARE, and both Mermaid diagrams parse.

Every number from that run is labelled as a pilot figure to re-measure on the lab machine.
